# Phase 7 Draft — Pattern Recognition & Corrective Injection

**Status:** DRAFT for Sotirios review. Not ready to execute. Two questions
at the bottom need your rulings first.

## What this phase does, in plain language

Right now, Praxis looks at posture photos and identifies **four** things:
- Forward head
- Scapular control problems
- Thoracic (mid-back) issues
- Hip stability

When it sees one of these, it adds correctives to your program to work on
them. That's the "focus tag" system from Phase 4.

Phase 7 expands this in two ways:

1. **Detect more patterns** — the four current ones are a start, but a
   trainer looking at a client can see more than that. What else can the
   app reliably see from a phone photo?

2. **Actively fix gaps** — right now if the program's already balanced,
   great. But if someone has (say) tight hips and the program doesn't
   already have hip mobility work in it, the app should recognize that
   and either add it or ask the user if they want it added.

That's the whole phase in two sentences: **see more, do more about what
you see.**

## Why this matters for real users

Right now a beginner shows up, takes photos, and gets a training plan.
The plan is good because the engine is good. But the plan is mostly the
same shape for everyone with similar equipment and experience — the four
focus tags nudge it, but they don't reshape it heavily.

With Phase 7, someone with (say) anterior pelvic tilt gets a program that
looks visibly different from someone without it. More hip flexor stretching
built in, more posterior chain work, maybe less overhead pressing until the
tilt clears. And when the tilt clears at retest, the program shifts back.

That's what "the app noticed and adapted" actually looks like. It's the
surprise moment you keep asking for — but built into the training, not
just the copy.

## What the plan would build

### Piece 1: Expand the focus-tag system

Right now the pose analyzer measures certain body landmarks (like ear
position relative to shoulders for forward head) and creates a "focus tag"
when a measurement crosses a threshold.

Phase 7 adds new measurements and new tags. The candidates I've drafted
are below — you rule on which ones are honest.

Each new tag would follow the same pattern as existing tags:
- A specific measurement from the photo
- A threshold for "this needs attention"
- A retest cadence
- A retirement rule (measurement clears → tag retires → correctives
  reallocate)

### Piece 2: Corrective injection when programs are missing something

Right now if you have a focus tag, the program has correctives added to
address it. Phase 7 makes this smarter:

If the engine generates your weekly program and it happens to already
have work that addresses your focus tag, great, leave it alone. But if
the generated program doesn't naturally have (say) hip mobility work and
you have a hip stability focus tag, the engine either injects the work
automatically or asks you first.

Which of those two behaviors is up to you (question 2 below).

### Piece 3: Show the user why

Whenever the engine injects a corrective because of a detected pattern,
the user sees the reason. Not clinical language — coaching language.

Example: *"Added glute bridges this week — your assessment showed some hip
stability we're building up. This one clears fast when you stay consistent."*

That's the transparency the whole app has been designed for. Nothing
happens without a reason the user can see.

