## Question 2 (for you to rule on) — How aggressive should the app be about adding correctives?

When the engine sees a pattern and the program doesn't already address it,
what should happen?

Option A: **Just add it automatically.** Corrective work shows up in the
program without asking. User sees a note explaining why.
- Pro: Users don't have to make a decision
- Con: Some users don't want more things added to their plan without say

Option B: **Ask the user first, once per pattern.** Prompt shows up:
"We noticed [X]. Want us to add corrective work for it? [Yes] [No, I'll
handle it]." The user's answer sticks. If they say yes, the corrective
comes in. If they say no, we don't ask again about that pattern until
their next assessment.
- Pro: Respects user agency, matches the Sacrifice/Test/Modify pattern
- Con: One more decision to make

Option C: **Frequency-based.** Add correctives but limit how many new
things per week. Like: no more than 2 new correctives injected per week
regardless of how many patterns are detected. If more are needed, they
queue for next week.
- Pro: Prevents overwhelming a beginner
- Con: Some patterns might wait longer than they should

Sotirios: ___

My recommendation is **Option B** because it matches the way the app
already treats users (asks, doesn't decide). But you might have a better
read from your practice — if in real training, clients trust you to just
add what they need, Option A might actually feel more like real coaching.

## What Phase 7 does NOT do

To keep this phase focused, it does not include:

- Any changes to how photos are captured (Phase 4's protocol stays)
- Any changes to the training program generation logic beyond corrective
  injection
- New assessment types (e.g. video, movement screens) — photo-based only
- Anything related to nutrition, sleep, or non-movement factors
- Real-time posture correction during exercises

## Estimated scope

If you rule YES on all patterns and pick Option B for injection: ~2 weeks
of code work, with the biggest chunk being the pose analyzer updates and
the associated tests. If you rule YES on fewer patterns, less time.

## Sequencing recommendation

- **Phase 8 (Progress Made Visible) first** — surfaces what already
  exists, small, fast, high emotional payoff for real users testing.
- **Phase 7 after Phase 8** — bigger, more clinical, but more valuable
  once you have real users generating photos to test the new detections
  against.

Order matters here because Phase 7's new detections will need real photos
to validate. Waiting until you have Motion Care clients on the app means
you can test Phase 7 against real bodies, not synthetic personas.

